{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And Zillah, she too.</b> She was not naturally childbearing, but was exclusively his for intercourse, and he would sit in her shadow constantly; she bore, she too, against his will, for the Holy One, blessed be He, wished to bring forth seed from her, and Lemeḥ's efforts were of no avail, and from him issued one who sharpens every forger of copper and iron." "<b>Sharpens every forger of copper and iron.</b> The sense of the verse is לוטש וחורש — he sharpens and forges — every copper and iron, that is, he hones and polishes craft tools. And the word נחשת in this place is what is called אציי״ר, whose joining with iron turns out well, as the craftsmen among us practice with small knives that are of copper called אציי״ר, mixed with iron. But the copper called ארא״ם — its joining with iron does not turn out well, and this is what the verse says: ״כולם סרי סוררים הולכי רכיל נחשת וברזל כולם משחיתים המה״ — all of them rebellious departures, going about with calumny of copper and iron; all of them corrupters are they. And this is called ארא״ם, because the prophet was reproving them and condemning them for the trait of calumny, for they would whisper to one another in calumny, and it would befall them like the case of copper and iron: for that union will never come to a good result, just as it is impossible to produce anything from copper and iron, for no craftsman can make a tool from their mixture, since all goes to ruin; therefore the verse concludes: ״כולם משחיתים המה״. And you will further find regarding this copper called ארא״ם that if you mix it with iron, besides the fact that no work at all can be made from it, it is impossible to separate one from the other, as craftsmen separate silver from gold and gold from silver. And the verse comes to allude in הולכי רכיל that whoever has calumny habituated on his tongue — this craft is difficult to distinguish and to separate from him, that evil practice having already become habitual in him. And the fact that it is called by the name נחשת — the name itself informs us of its matter. And both are called by the name נחשת because it is from the matter of testing (נסיון), as it is written (Genesis 30:27): ״נחשתי ויברכני ה׳ בגללך״, and no craftsman in the world can identify whether it is אציי״ר or iron until he brings it to the test of trial; and if it breaks, he knows it is אציי״ר, and if it bends and does not break, then he knows it is iron (ברז״ל)." "<b>And the sister of Tuval-Qayin was Na'amah.</b> Our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: she was the wife of Noaḥ, and she was so called because her deeds were pleasant (נאים) and sweet (נעימים). And some say she was the wife of Ashmedon, the mother of Ashmedai, and the demons were born from her. And there were four women who were the mothers of demons: Lilith, Na'amah, Igrat, and Maḥalat. And each and every one of them has camps and troops of the spirit of impurity without number. And they say that each one rules during one of the four seasons of the year, and they gather on Mount Noshaf, near the Mountains of Darkness. And each one rules in her season from the hour of sunset until midnight — they and all their camps. And over all of them Solomon ruled, and he called them עבדים ושפחות — male and female servants — and he would use them at his will. And these four women are the wives of the prince of Esav; and in his likeness, Esav married four women, as detailed in the Torah. And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, also elaborated in the Midrash concerning the matter of the first Adam, that all those one hundred and thirty (ק״ל) years during which he was under ban for his sin, he begot spirits (רוחין) and demons (שדין) and lilin (לילין) and harming spirits (מזיקין). And this is a distinction and perfection in the species of man: that from him come the spiritual beings within the spheres of the elements, and from him too there are intellectual spiritual beings on high. And understand this."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {2 "ק״ל expanded as 130; שרו של עשו rendered 'the prince of Esav' (angelic prince, per rabbinic usage)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:10:33.698614847Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}